// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// The wreck — a coaster lying across the beach, blocking the walk. You
// get past it by going through it: in at the breach in the near side,
// up the stair out of the hold, across the deck, and down the ramp of
// torn plate on the far side.
//
// Collision is a floor lookup, not a solid: shipFloorAt() returns the
// highest walkable surface at or just below your feet, so the hold floor
// and the deck can sit above one another at the same (x, z) without the
// two fighting. Anywhere inside the hull with no floor is solid, and you
// get pushed back out the way you came.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
const SHIP_X = 6, SHIP_Z = -155;
/* THE SECOND CACHE IS PLACED, not rolled. `mounds[1]` is the one with the CD
 * in it, and where a thing is found is half of what it means — so it sits
 * halfway between the wreck and Bradley rather than wherever the spacing
 * happened to drop it. It has to be done HERE and not where the mounds are
 * built: `SHIP_Z` is declared further down the file, and reaching for it up
 * there is a temporal dead zone that takes the whole page with it. Bradley
 * stands ten metres past `mounds[0]`, which is why that is the number. */
if (mounds.length > 1) {
  const bradleyZ = mounds[0].z - 10;
  const m = mounds[1];
  m.z = (SHIP_Z + bradleyZ) / 2;
  m.x = 9.8 + mulberry32(4411)() * (CLIFF_BLOCK_X - 11.4);
  m.root.position.set(m.x, 0, m.z);
}
const shipwreck = makeShipwreck({ sandMat });
shipwreck.position.set(SHIP_X, 0, SHIP_Z);
scene.add(shipwreck);
/* AND THE BIGGEST THING ON THE BEACH WAS DRAWN FROM A KILOMETRE AWAY, EVERY
 * FRAME, FOR THE WHOLE WALK. She is 158 meshes and she was in no far list at
 * all, and `cullBeyondFog` deliberately exempts anything bigger than
 * `SKYLINE_R` — because culling a landform does not remove a dim object, it
 * opens a hole in the fog — so the one rule that would have caught her was
 * the one rule written to leave her alone. Measured: 162 draw calls at EVERY
 * station down the walk, from the spawn to the fence, which at the pavilion
 * is a third of the frame and at the tower is half of it. A twenty-eight
 * metre hull is a skyline object at two hundred metres and nothing at all at
 * two hundred and forty: `FogExp2` 0.014 leaves 0.007 % of her there. She
 * goes in the list beside her own drift.
 *
 * AND SHE IS MERGED BY MATERIAL, so what it costs to stand on her deck is
 * seventeen draws and not a hundred and sixty-two — `bakeStatic`'s own rule,
 * that this is for BUILDINGS, and she is the biggest one in the game. Her
 * hull quads carry `noBake` (they are lifted off their own maps and must
 * keep them) and every named part is held by something, so both are left
 * exactly where they are. */
farProps.push({ obj: shipwreck, z: SHIP_Z, d: 240 });
/* on a warm step, so it runs after everything this file hangs on her — the
 * gear, the head, the catch, the door — has been built */
warmStep('wreck merge', () => { bakeStatic(shipwreck); collectDetail(shipwreck); });
/* ---- FINE DETAIL IS DRAWN ONLY WHERE IT CAN BE SEEN. What the merge cannot
 * take is everything somebody holds — the helm's spokes, the lantern, the
 * fittings of the hold — and it was a hundred separate draws seen from the
 * crate by Bradly, 115 m away through the fog, where every one of them is a
 * pixel or less. A mesh under 0.6 m across is drawn only inside
 * 70 x its radius + 10 m of the camera: a 0.2 m bolt goes at 24 m, a
 * 0.6 m cleat at 52. Only UNNAMED meshes whose own visibility nothing else
 * writes, so this never argues with the game about what is there. */
